rocesses  of this sort um he said fine let the let  the committee uh investigate this look  at it  but obviously they're going to want to  take evidence from him they're going to  want to talk to him and we should let  that process run its course and not get  ahead of ourselves  um we could talk about that um all day  but of course there was another couple  of things that i want to ask you about  not least  the  rail strike rail and maritime union  voting to go on a national strike um  you could i think i'm right in saying  consider legislation to ensure minimum  staffing levels in the event of a strike  that was something that was in your  manifesto is that something that you  would uh implement  well look obviously grant shaps is  working quite closely at the moment with  um you know the rail unions to try to  make sure that they don't go  this step um you know the truth is the  pay settlements haven't yet um you know  even been discussed there are some  important reforms and changes that are  going to need to be  made on things like ticket offices some  of which aren't really  used anymore and  they're not it's not viable to keep them  all open there are digital technologies  that we can use to make processes work  better to help passengers there are  technological things that will improve  rail safety but my question is would the  government consider changing the law to  ensure minimum staffing levels as was  suggested in your manifesto well of  course we don't rule any of these things  out but at the moment what we want to  try to do is to to work with the unions  and to get some constructive engagement  here with them there are some real  challenges facing the railways during  the pandemic huge amounts of public  subsidy had to go to keep the rail  network running with passenger numbers  down passenger numbers you know remain  down and that means we are going to have  to change the way certain things work  and change certain practices  we're not ruling anything out but but  you know um what i'm trying to say okay  is in the first instance in these things  you know obviously we try to engage  constructively you know with the rail  unions and the workforce in each of  these cases final question before i let  you go uh ukraine last night pleading  with the royal navy  to go  to uh odessa to try to stop that  blockade of the port we were speaking to  your colleague yesterday he said that at  grand chaps of course he said that if  that blockade continues more people are  likely to die around the world from  starvation than will die in the war in  ukraine are we looking again at the  royal navy going well look um there  aren't any plans to send the the royal  navy to the black sea however what we  what we are looking at is is other uh  corridors to try to get some of those um  grain stores out of ukraine  i understand there's sort of perhaps 30  to 40 million tons of of grain there  and that's um you know a significant  proportion of the current world stocks  which is probably around 230 million  tons it's very important in these  situations when you have  this kind of stress on the system that  you keep world markets open so that you  can move grain from the countries that  have it in store to those so overland is  that what you're saying uh yes but for  other yeah exactly so uh countries that  have put in place export bans for  instance that's very  you know damaging to confidence around  the world luckily all of the big  producer nations recognize this and  they're all keeping their markets open  so ourselves the european union the  united states we're keeping markets open  so that grain can flow around the world  but i know that there is some thought  being given by the g7 as to how we can  uh potentially get safe corridors to be  able to move uh some of those uh some of  those grain stores out uh by land okay  for now thanks very much indeed minister  much appreciated thank you it's going to  be a busy day for you uh now 